+# Condition C: example reuse with independent masks
+
+This experiment extends branch `sudoku-a-e-options`, base commit
+`fe967e2bbb613ed5a1aab903e7c5e111d159b0a6`, with proposal condition C.
+The comparison preserves the completed A/F experiments' model, data, training
+budget, and evaluation protocol. Each job records its actual source state and
+resolved configuration; the base commit alone does not describe the C changes.
+
+## Training contract
+
+C retains a puzzle across the same four scheduled states as E/F, but samples
+a fresh uniform subset of target positions at every transition. Previously
+visible target cells can become masked again. Givens remain visible, and all
+visible target cells contain their training labels. Replacement occurs when a
+slot wraps to the first stage. No confidence threshold or label-dependent
+ordering selects C's masks.
+
+The stored schedule is `(81, 61, 41, 21)` with reference length 81. As in the
+existing E/F implementation, these are fractions of the initially empty cells,
+not literal mask counts over a separate 81-cell solution target. For a puzzle
+with 57 empty cells the counts are 57, 43, 29, and 15. Slots begin at balanced,
+randomly shuffled stages. Initial states use independent uniform subsets; the
+first partially completed cycle can therefore have fewer than four updates.
+
+| Setting | Value |
+|---|---|
+| Seeds | 1000, 2000, 3000, 4000, 5000 |
+| Model | Bidirectional Transformer, 8 layers, width 256, MLP 768, 8 attention/KV heads |
+| Vocabulary / sequence length | 11 / 81 |
+| Dropout / RMSNorm epsilon | 0 / 1e-6 |
+| Training | 2 epochs, 53,570 optimizer updates |
+| Batch | 32 per GPU × 2 GPUs = 64 |
+| Optimizer | AdamW, learning rate 3e-4, weight decay 0.01 |
+| Learning rate schedule | Cosine, 1000 warmup updates |
+| Gradient norm clipping | 1 |
+| EMA | 0.9999 |
+| Forward precision | bf16 autocast |
+| Training data | PUMA Sudoku files, 1,804,462 source training puzzles |
+| Validation-loss split | 5% of training data, split seed 2026 |
+| Generative evaluation | First 999 puzzles of the supplied 99,999-puzzle test set |
+| Evaluation interval | Every 1000 updates and update 53,570 |
+| Saved checkpoints | Every 10,000 updates and update 53,570 |
+| Primary decoder | `top_k_unmasking_1`, greedy, temperature 0 |
+| Additional decoders | `top_k` and `top_k_margin`, each revealing 1, 2, or 3 cells |
+| Evaluation batch | 16 per rank, hard-coded in the inherited evaluator |
+| Evaluation weights | EMA for generation; non-EMA for random-mask validation loss |
+
+The loss averages each example's masked-token negative log probabilities over
+its current masked positions, then averages across the batch. An optimizer
+update trains one batch of current states; a four-state trajectory is four
+updates for that slot. Standard training uses a fresh batch each update, while
+C/E/F consume fewer fresh examples. Identical seed labels pair runs for analysis;
+they do not establish identical random-mask streams or equal data exposure.
+
+## Launch and completion
+
+Use the existing environment/data setup scripts and precompute the Sudoku
+caches before submitting the full study. The Slurm launcher targets partition
+`cscc-gpu-p`, account `cscc-users`, and QoS `cscc-gpu-qos`. The observed account
+limits are four submitted jobs, two running jobs, and four running GPUs.
+`scripts/run_sudoku_C_array.sbatch` therefore submits two array tasks with
+two GPUs each: task 0 runs seeds 1000, 3000, and 5000 sequentially; task 1 runs
+2000 and 4000. All five seeds are included in one submission, with at most two
+seeds training concurrently.
+
+```bash
+mkdir -p runs/logs
+EXP_DIR=runs/sudoku_C_20261022 sbatch scripts/run_sudoku_C_array.sbatch
+```
+
+The launcher rejects smoke overrides and per-run timeouts for the full study.
+After each successful seed, it re-scores the final EMA checkpoint with the three
+precision modes below, using the primary decoder. `DONE` marks successful
+training, while `RESCORE_DONE` records successful completion of all three
+re-scores. Training results and re-score completion remain separate statuses.
+
+After the array terminates, `scripts/rescore_sudoku_C_curve.sbatch` evaluates
+the saved EMA checkpoints at updates 10,000, 20,000, 30,000, 40,000, and 50,000
+in full fp32 for all five seeds. Together with the final 53,570-update score,
+these give the same six checkpoint positions used in the B/D report. The job
+validates checkpoint, source, and data hashes before reusing existing scores.
+`CURVE_RESCORE_DONE` requires all six scores for a seed. Submit the CPU analysis
+after this job so the exported precision table includes the full corrected curve.
+
+```bash
+EXP_DIR=runs/sudoku_C_20261022 sbatch --dependency=afterany:335458 scripts/rescore_sudoku_C_curve.sbatch
+# Use the returned curve job ID for the analysis dependency.
+EXP_DIR=runs/sudoku_C_20261022 sbatch --dependency=afterany:<curve_job_id> scripts/finalize_sudoku_C.sbatch
+```
+
+`scripts/smoke_sudoku_C.sbatch` checks 61 training updates on a separate cache
+of 4096 training records and 999 test records. Its validation ratio of 0.02
+selects 19 puzzles; it checks all six decoders and an EMA checkpoint. Smoke
+artifacts use a separate experiment directory and are excluded from the full
+53,570-update analysis. A submitted or queued full-study job does not count as
+a completed experiment.
+
+## Evaluation precision and baseline provenance
+
+The teammates' [shared results report](https://forge.coreweave.com/wandb/puma-mbz/puma-sudoku/reports/Sudoku-progression-conditions-final-accuracy-and-the-bf16-eval-issue--VmlldzoxODE2MjE5Ng)
+documents a precision issue in the inherited decoder. The sharing token is
+intentionally excluded from this repository link.
+
+`sampling.mdm_sampling` computes softmax outside autocast on bf16 forward
+logits, leaving its output probabilities in bf16. Near-one confidence values
+can round to ties and change the reveal order. The report observes large effects
+for some B/D seeds. Its observed preference for lower indices is not a general
+`torch.topk` guarantee: PyTorch documents that tied indices need not be stable.
+
+Training-time evaluation remains the legacy implementation so C's full curve
+is directly comparable to the available A/F curves. Final EMA checkpoint
+re-scores are separate artifacts with explicit modes:
+
+- `legacy_bf16`: inherited bf16 forward and confidence softmax.
+- `bf16_fp32_softmax`: bf16 forward with `F.softmax(logits.float(), dim=-1)`.
+- `full_fp32`: fp32 forward and confidence softmax.
+
+Checkpoint re-scoring uses inference mode, matching the training-time EMA
+evaluation context. The runtime uses the project's pinned PyTorch 2.13 build;
+the external API links below describe the current published API, while the
+installed API docstrings were also checked during implementation.
+
+These are distinct numerical protocols. The report's B/D re-scores use full
+fp32 and exist at only six saved checkpoints. A/F checkpoints were not rescored
+in that report. The reported fixed-softmax results were within 1.2 percentage
+points of full fp32 on 11 final checkpoints; that observation does not establish
+equivalence. Do not pool these protocols, replace legacy learning curves with
+isolated re-scores, or infer 1000-update crossing times from six checkpoint scores.
+
+The separate full-fp32 comparison uses the exact B/D final observations saved
+in `results/sudoku_baselines/bd_full_fp32.json`. The downloaded report artifacts
+do not contain their exact intermediate histories, so C-versus-B/D comparisons
+are restricted to update 53,570. B/D retrospective evaluation hardware, batch
+layout, TF32 settings, and test-prefix hashes were not recorded in those artifacts.
+The script preserves these limits and reports missing C observations explicitly.
+
+```bash
+python3 scripts/compare_sudoku_c_fp32.py \
+  --c-root runs/sudoku_C_20261022 \
+  --out results/sudoku_C_fp32_comparison
+```
+
+The committed baseline records are:
+
+- A: `results/sudoku/raw/sudoku_AE_20261016/A/seed<s>/`.
+- F: `results/sudoku/raw/sudoku_PF_20261017/F/seed<s>/`.
+
+All five runs of each condition reached 53,570 updates. Their legacy primary
+accuracy means and sample standard deviations are A **88.108 ± 1.760%** and
+F **95.736 ± 1.259%**. The copied records contain successful exits in
+`run_info.txt`; they do not contain checkpoint tensors or necessarily `DONE`.
+Original A/F checkpoint paths in metadata are relative
+`runs/sudoku_AE_20261016/A/seed<s>` and
+`runs/sudoku_PF_20261017/F/seed<s>`; the report does not establish their owning
+home directory. W&B run IDs use `A-seed<s>-v2` and `F-seed<s>-v2`.
+
+## Analysis
+
+Run the standard-library comparison from the repository root, replacing the C
+experiment path with the actual output directory:
+
+```bash
+python3 scripts/compare_sudoku_c.py \
+  --c-root runs/sudoku_C_20261022 \
+  --out results/sudoku_C_comparison
+```
+
+The command accepts an experiment root containing `C/seed<s>/`, a root
+containing `seed<s>/` directly, or individual seed directories. Re-running it
+updates the analysis from the current logs. Missing/active seeds remain explicit
+rows; an incomplete run's last observed accuracy is never labeled final.
+Duplicate attempts for a condition/seed are rejected instead of pooled.
+
+Final scores require a successful completion marker or recorded exit 0 and an
+evaluation at the exact update budget. Final means include completed seeds only,
+with their count reported; use all five completed seeds for the main result.
+Standard deviations use `ddof=1`. Partial runs may already establish threshold
+crossings, but threshold means are omitted unless all requested seeds reach
+that target. Crossing requires two consecutive scheduled evaluations at or
+above 50%, 75%, or 90%, and is assigned to the first evaluation. Confirmation
+steps are recorded separately. A last-checkpoint-only crossing is unconfirmed;
+a missing intermediate evaluation cannot confirm a crossing across the gap.
+
+Outputs are `per_seed.csv`, `evals.csv`, `paired.csv`, `summary.json`, and
+`summary.md`. The per-seed file records input hashes, final and observed scores,
+target attainment, train/evaluation timing, and available run provenance.
+Paired differences are C minus A/F; positive accuracy differences favor C,
+while negative update differences indicate fewer C updates. Train time excludes
+evaluation; the sum of measured evaluation durations is reported separately.
+Recorded start/end wall time also includes launch and checkpoint overhead.
+Cross-node timing is observational unless hardware and resource allocation match.
+
+To add separate final re-scores:
+
+```bash
+python3 scripts/compare_sudoku_c.py \
+  --c-root runs/sudoku_C_20261022 \
+  --rescore runs/sudoku_C_20261022 \
+  --out results/sudoku_C_comparison
+```
+
+Directory inputs to `--rescore` select `rescore_*.json`; explicit files are also
+accepted. Each file must use the `checkpoint_rescore` schema from
+`scripts/rescore_sudoku_checkpoint.py`: condition C, seed, checkpoint update,
+EMA flag, precision mode, decoder metrics, measured evaluation time, and
+evaluation metadata. They produce `precision_rescores.csv` and separate summary
+entries, without modifying the primary A/C/F comparison. No W&B uploads or
+training runs are created by the analysis command.
+
+## Departures from the proposal
+
+The completed team experiments use eight model layers rather than six, five
+seeds rather than three, a single 81-cell grid with visible givens rather than
+a separate puzzle prompt and full solution target, and a schedule scaled to
+the empty cells. Generative accuracy is checked on a fixed subset of the supplied
+test set during training; the separate held-out training split supplies validation
+loss only. This is not an untouched final test evaluation. The evaluator checks
+Sudoku constraints and givens, rather than exact equality to the stored solution.
+
+Validation-loss masks are sampled from a common distribution, rather than a
+fixed shared set of masks. Duplicate removal across splits, separate validation puzzles for generative
+evaluation, per-cell accuracy, random-order decoding, and complete data-exposure
+accounting were not established by the inherited experiment records. They are
+not retroactively claimed here. The controlled C comparison preserves the
+existing protocol rather than changing these choices for C alone.
+
+Protocol sources: the repository and raw run records at
+the base commit, the teammates' report, the [PUMA paper](https://arxiv.org/html/2602.10314v2),
+and the official [softmax](https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/2.14/generated/torch.nn.functional.softmax.html)
+and [topk](https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/2.14/generated/torch.topk.html) API documentation.
